<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Bengal</h1><p class="st-meta">1627 CE – 1825 CE · capital Chinsurah</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Dutch East India Company's directorate in Bengal, based at Chinsurah on the Hooghly, a major source of silk, opium and saltpetre.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>) began trading in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in the 1620s, opening a factory at Pipli and then establishing its main base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinsurah">Chinsurah</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooghly-river">Hooghly</a> river around 1635. In 1656 it built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-gustavus">Fort Gustavus</a> there, and Bengal became a separate directorate with posts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasimbazar">Kasimbazar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> supplied raw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth, saltpetre for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> and opium for the Indonesian trade, and by the late 17th century was among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC's</a> most profitable regions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> operated under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nawabate-of-bengal">Nawabate of Bengal</a>, paying duties and seeking privileges by negotiation.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company's</a> victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinsurah">Chinsurah</a> in 1759 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ambitions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. The British occupied the posts during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, and Chinsurah was ceded to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1825 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dutch-bengal.jpg" alt="Dutch Bengal" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dutch_East_India_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1627 CE – 1825 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chinsurah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Church</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch, Bengali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dutch Chinsurah, Directorate of Bengal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1726&amp;focus=dutch-bengal" aria-label="Dutch Bengal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7043 1333 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dutch Bengal lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7363 1589L5097 634M7363 1589L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5097 634h0M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7363 1589h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7363 1589h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chinsurah. <a href="/atlas?year=1726&amp;focus=dutch-bengal">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

